          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When is standby the right class?</h3>

          <p>
            Standby is the only class that works while nobody is home. That matters for a sump pump under a finished basement, for a freezer full of meat while you are on vacation, and for an elderly parent who should not be dragging a 200-pound machine across a wet lawn. On a natural gas line or a large propane tank, it is also the only class that never needs refueling.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Conventional or inverter portable?</h3>

          <p>
            Conventional portables win on watts per dollar: an 8,000-watt open-frame unit costs about the same as a 4,500-watt inverter. You pay in noise and fuel. The engine runs at one fixed speed whether it is powering a refrigerator or a whole panel, so it burns gasoline at a similar rate either way. Inverter portables throttle down as demand falls, which is why a small inverter can run overnight on a tank while an open frame cannot.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where do battery stations fit?</h3>

          <p>
            Battery stations are the only option that is safe to run indoors. They shine in short outages, with overnight medical loads and in apartments, but their capacity is finite. A 1kWh station runs a modern refrigerator for most of a day, and a 3.6kWh unit buys a couple of days of essentials. Pair one with solar or a small inverter generator outside and you have a quiet, efficient hybrid. Our <a href="/best-portable-power-station/">portable power station rankings</a> dig into capacity and chemistry. For a circuit-by-circuit outage plan built around batteries, read our guide to the <a href="/best-portable-power-station-for-home-backup/">best power stations for home backup</a>, and for recharging off the grid, see our <a href="/best-solar-generator/">solar generator pairings</a>.
          </p>
