        <div id="connecting" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. How Do You Safely Connect a Generator to Your House?
          </h2>

          <p>
            There are exactly three safe ways to bring generator power into a home. The first is heavy-duty outdoor extension cords run from the generator straight to individual appliances. The second is a <strong>manual transfer switch</strong> installed beside your panel by an electrician, with a power inlet box outside. The third is a listed <strong>interlock kit</strong> fitted to your main panel by an electrician, again with an inlet. Standby units use an automatic transfer switch that does the same job without anyone touching it.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">NEVER BACKFEED</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Never plug a generator into a wall outlet or a dryer receptacle.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A cord with a male plug on both ends, sometimes called a suicide cord, pushes generator power backward through your panel and out onto the utility lines, where the pole transformer steps it up to thousands of volts. Line workers fixing what they believe is a dead line can be electrocuted, and neighbors can be injured. It also energizes your house wiring with no main breaker protection and can start a fire when grid power comes back. It breaks the National Electrical Code and is illegal in most jurisdictions.</p>
          </div>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why does a transfer switch or interlock matter?</h3>

          <p>
            Every legal connection shares one feature: isolation. A transfer switch or interlock physically guarantees that the house is fed by the utility or by the generator, never both at the same moment. That protects line crews, protects the generator from destruction when the grid returns, and protects your wiring. It is not an optional extra; it is what lets a portable safely run hardwired loads like a furnace or a well pump, which extension cords cannot reach.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What does a transfer switch or interlock cost?</h3>

          <p>
            Prices vary by region. A manual transfer switch covering six to ten chosen circuits, plus an outdoor inlet and a permit, commonly costs $800 to $1,800 installed. An interlock kit is cheaper, often $500 to $1,200 installed, and lets you power any circuit in the panel provided you manage the load yourself. Most places require a permit for either, and both belong in the hands of a licensed electrician. Match the inlet to the generator: a 30-amp L14-30 suits most mid-size portables, and the largest need a 50-amp inlet.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How do you use extension cords safely?</h3>

          <p>
            Choose outdoor-rated cords of 12-gauge or heavier, sized for the load, and keep them as short as you practically can. Never route them through a door or window that then has to stay propped open, because that gap is exactly where exhaust gets in; a cord port in an exterior wall or a properly installed inlet is the better answer. Newer portables with GFCI-protected outlets sometimes trip when connected through a transfer switch. That is a neutral-bonding issue for your electrician to sort out, not a reason to defeat the protection. A <a href="/best-whole-house-surge-protector/">whole-house surge protector</a> is worth adding during the same visit, and a <a href="/best-circuit-breaker-finder/">circuit breaker finder</a> speeds up choosing the right circuits for a transfer switch.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="carbon-monoxide" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. How Do You Prevent Carbon Monoxide Poisoning From a Generator?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Run a fuel-burning generator outdoors only, at least 20 feet from the house with the exhaust aimed away, and back that up with battery-powered CO alarms and a generator that has an automatic CO shutoff. Portable generators are among the leading causes of carbon monoxide deaths in the United States, and most of those deaths happen during and just after storms. One portable generator can produce as much carbon monoxide as hundreds of idling cars. The gas has no color or smell, and early symptoms such as headache, dizziness, nausea and fatigue are easily mistaken for flu or exhaustion. People who are asleep can die without ever waking up.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where can a generator run, and where can it never run?</h3>

          <p>
            The rules are short and absolute. A fuel-burning generator runs outdoors and nowhere else: not in the house, a basement, a crawlspace, an attached garage, a breezeway or a shed, and not in a garage with the door up, because fans and open doors do not clear the gas reliably. Federal safety agencies recommend at least 20 feet from the house, with the exhaust pointed away from doors, windows and vents. More distance is better and wind direction matters; the research behind that guidance shows exhaust can still drift inside at 20 feet in the wrong conditions.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Placement Numbers to Remember</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">0</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Safe indoor locations for an engine</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">20 ft+</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Portable to house, exhaust pointed away</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">5 ft</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Typical standby clearance to windows and vents</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">Every level</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Battery-backed CO alarms, near bedrooms</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Which CO alarms work in an outage?</h3>

          <p>
            Only alarms that do not rely on grid power: battery-only units, or plug-ins with a battery backup, placed on every level and outside every sleeping area. Test them before storm season starts. If an alarm goes off or anyone feels ill, get everyone into fresh air right away and call 911. Do not go back inside to hunt for the source.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Does an automatic CO shutoff make a generator safe?</h3>

          <p>
            It makes it safer, so buy one that has it. Honda calls the feature CO-MINDER, Champion uses CO Shield, Generac uses COsense, and other brands have their own names; the usual reference standards are ANSI/PGMA G300 and UL 2201. These systems stop the engine when CO builds up around the unit, which guards against the most common fatal error, running in a garage or too close to the house. They are a backstop, not permission to cut corners on placement. Standby generators are installed to the maker's clearance rules, commonly 5 feet from openings, and inspected under a permit, one more reason they are the safest fuel-burning choice.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What do you do when it rains?</h3>

          <p>
            Weather is the trap, because rain is what tempts people to move a generator into the garage. Instead, buy a purpose-made generator tent or a ventilated enclosure rated for use while running, or build a simple open-sided canopy well away from the house. And never refuel a hot engine: let it cool first so gasoline cannot spill onto a hot muffler.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="fuel-plan" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. How Much Fuel Should You Store for a Generator?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Store enough for three days of intermittent running, which for a mid-size portable is usually 15 to 25 gallons of stabilized gasoline or several propane cylinders. Fuel is what runs out first in a regional outage: gas stations need electricity to pump, and the ones that have it draw long lines. Plan for your longest likely outage at a realistic load, not the brochure run time at 25 percent load.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How fast does a generator burn fuel?</h3>

          <p>
            Mid-size conventional portables commonly use somewhere around half a gallon to a gallon of gasoline an hour at moderate load, which adds up to 12 to 24 gallons a day if they run around the clock. Most owners do not run them that way. Running the generator for two hours out of every four to six keeps a refrigerator and freezer cold and a sump pit in check while cutting fuel use dramatically. A small inverter at light load can run all night on a single gallon.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How long does stored gasoline last?</h3>

          <p>
            Not long. Untreated pump gas starts to break down within a month or two, and ethanol blends pull in water. Add stabilizer when you fill the can, rotate the stored fuel into your car every few months, and buy ethanol-free gas if you can find it nearby. Keep it in approved containers in a detached shed or garage, away from ignition sources and never in the living space. Local fire codes often limit household gasoline storage, commonly to around 25 gallons, so check the rule where you live.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Is propane or natural gas easier?</h3>

          <p>
            Propane is far easier to stockpile. It does not go stale, standard 20-pound cylinders are sold at hardware stores, and a dual fuel portable lets you switch over when gasoline gets scarce. Expect about 10 percent less output on propane, and be aware that small cylinders can struggle to vaporize fast enough under heavy load in deep cold. Natural gas, which most standby units burn, rarely fails during electrical outages, which is why a gas-fed standby can keep running for days.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">RULE OF THUMB</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Keep three days of fuel for intermittent running.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">For a mid-size portable run two hours on and four hours off, that usually means 15 to 25 gallons of stabilized gasoline or several propane cylinders. If your outages regularly outlast that supply, the numbers are pointing you toward a standby unit on natural gas or a large propane tank.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="real-cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. How Much Does a Home Generator Really Cost?
          </h2>

          <p>
            A $1,200 portable becomes a $2,500 to $3,500 system once it is connected and equipped, and a whole-home 20 to 24kW standby unit commonly lands between $10,000 and $20,000 installed. Once connection, fuel and upkeep are counted, the sticker price is the smallest number in the decision.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What does a portable system cost?</h3>

          <p>
            The portable is only the start. Add an inlet with a transfer switch or interlock and a permit, a heavy cord, fuel cans, stabilizer, a cover and better CO alarms, and the total reaches that $2,500 to $3,500 range. That is still far below standby, and for most homes with gas heat it is the best value on this list.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What does standby cost installed?</h3>

          <p>
            Standby works on a different scale. The unit itself is roughly a third to a half of the bill; the rest covers the automatic transfer switch, electrical work, gas piping and sometimes a bigger gas meter, the pad, permits and inspection. Long gas runs, propane tank sets and electrical service upgrades push the total above the usual $10,000 to $20,000 range. Our <a href="/generac-generator-cost/">Generac cost breakdown</a> goes through each line of a typical quote.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What does it cost to keep running?</h3>

          <p>
            Standby units need a service visit every year, typically $200 to $400 on a dealer plan, plus a new battery every few years, and their weekly self-exercise burns a small amount of fuel. Portables need an oil change after the first few hours and then on a schedule tied to run hours, along with plugs and air filters. The fuel you rotate into your car is not really wasted.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Is a generator worth the money?</h3>

          <p>
            Set the cost against what an outage costs you. A flooded finished basement can cost more than a standby installation, and a freezer of meat, a week in a hotel, lost remote-work income or a burst frozen pipe are all real money. If two or three of those events have hit you in the past five years, a generator is not a luxury. If your outages are four hours once a summer, a battery station and a cooler are the rational answer.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="readiness" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. How Do You Keep a Generator Ready for an Outage?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Follow a routine: run it monthly, manage the fuel, service it on schedule and rehearse the hookup before storm season. The most common generator failure is not mechanical at all. It is a machine that sat for a year with old fuel in the carburetor, where varnish from evaporated gasoline clogs the tiny passages of a small engine, so the unit that started fine in the store will not start in a storm.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">For portables</h3>

          <p>
            Start it once a month and run it for 15 to 20 minutes with a real load plugged in, such as a space heater or a work light. Either keep stabilized fuel in the tank or run the carburetor dry with the fuel valve closed before storage, whichever the manual recommends for your model. Change the oil after the break-in period the manual specifies and then by run hours, and check the level before every outage. If the unit does not charge its own electric-start battery, keep that battery on a maintainer.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">For standby units</h3>

          <p>
            Let the weekly exercise cycle run and watch the app or controller for warnings. Book a yearly service that covers oil, filters, plugs, a battery test and a transfer switch check. Keep mulch, snow drifts, shrubs and leaves clear of the enclosure so the cooling air intake can breathe. After any long outage, have the oil checked, because a multi-day run eats a large part of the service interval.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Do a dry run before storm season</h3>

          <p>
            Roll the portable out, connect it through the inlet, flip the interlock or transfer switch and bring the circuits on one at a time. That is how you discover the missing adapter, the dead battery or the cord that is too short on a sunny afternoon instead of at midnight in the rain. Keep a printed card by the panel listing the steps in order, and teach a second adult in the house to do it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="matching" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            11. Which Generator Is Best for Your Type of Home?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Match the pick to the house and the people in it. These five situations cover most homes, and each points to a different machine on the list.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Suburban house with gas heat and a basement</h3>

          <p>
            If multi-day outages come along now and then, the Champion dual fuel or the Generac GP8000E on an interlock or manual transfer switch covers the sump pump, furnace, refrigerator and freezer. This is the most common situation, and it is where the best value on the list sits.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Rural house on a well with long outages</h3>

          <p>
            The pump needs 240V, so a 120V-only inverter cannot be the main unit. The GP8000E or the DuroMax handles the pump, and the DuroMax has enough left for a window air conditioner. If you lose power for most of a week every year, get a price on the Guardian 22kW running from a propane tank. Small cabins and lighter loads are covered in our guide to the <a href="/best-small-generator-for-home/">best small generators for home</a>.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Southern home where heat is the danger</h3>

          <p>
            Central air dominates the load here, so the honest options are standby with load management, or a large portable with a soft-start kit on the condenser. A window unit in one bedroom, run from a mid-size portable, is a cheaper middle route that keeps people safe through a heat wave.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Apartment, condo or a medical device at home</h3>

          <p>
            Start with the EcoFlow Delta Pro or a smaller battery station. It runs safely indoors, and if outages drag on you can add a quiet inverter outside to recharge it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Close neighbors and short outages</h3>

          <p>
            The Honda EU2200i or the Westinghouse iGen4500 keeps the essentials running without much noise. If you would rather have plain gasoline and big output, compare our <a href="/best-gas-generator/">best gas generator</a> picks.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Common Mistakes With Outage Generators
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying by the big number on the box</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The headline figure on most packaging is starting watts, not what the machine can sustain all night. Build your list from running watts, add the largest single starting surge, and keep about 20 percent headroom. A generator held at its limit for hours wears quickly and sags voltage in ways motors dislike.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving out the transfer switch</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Extension cords handle a fridge and a lamp, but they cannot reach a furnace, a well pump or a hardwired sump pump. Plugging into a wall outlet to power those backfeeds the grid and can kill a line worker. Count an interlock or transfer switch, installed under a permit, as part of the generator's price.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running it in the garage to keep it dry</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Every storm season brings deaths from generators run in attached garages with the door open, because the gas reaches the house faster than people expect. Keep it outdoors, 20 feet or more from the house with the exhaust aimed away, under a ventilated cover in rain, and keep battery CO alarms working.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Storing it with stale gasoline</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Untreated fuel left in the tank for a year varnishes the carburetor, and the generator refuses to start when you need it. Add stabilizer every time you fill a can, run the unit monthly under load, and either keep the tank stabilized or run the carburetor dry as the manual directs.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying more generator than you can fuel</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                At heavy load a 12,000-watt portable can go through a lot of gasoline in a day, and stations shut down in regional outages. Before buying big, work out where three days of fuel will come from. If you cannot answer that, a smaller unit run in cycles, propane, or a natural gas standby is the better plan.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            13. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How big a generator do I need for an average house during an outage?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Essentials alone, meaning a refrigerator, freezer, lights, internet and a gas furnace blower, need about 3,500 to 5,000 running watts. A sump pump or a 240V well pump moves you into the 6,000 to 8,000 watt range. Central air plus the whole panel usually takes a 20 to 24kW standby unit or a smaller one with load management. Read your appliance data plates instead of trusting averages.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a standby generator worth it over a good portable?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                That turns on how often and how long your power goes out. Standby costs several times as much, but it starts itself, needs no refueling on natural gas and covers the whole house. Multi-day outages most years, a finished basement, or someone at home who cannot manage a portable usually justify it. For occasional outages, a portable with an interlock is much better value.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How far from the house should a portable generator be?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Federal safety guidance calls for at least 20 feet from the house, with the exhaust aimed away from doors, windows and vents, and more distance is better. Never run one in a garage, a carport against the house, a basement, a crawlspace or a shed, even with the doors open. Back up good placement with a CO shutoff generator and battery-powered CO alarms on every level.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I plug my generator into a dryer outlet to power the house?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. That is backfeeding, and it can electrocute utility workers, hurt neighbors, wreck your generator when grid power returns and start a fire. It also breaks electrical code. The legal routes to house circuits are a manual transfer switch or a listed panel interlock with an outdoor inlet, installed by a licensed electrician, or extension cords run straight to appliances.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will a portable generator run my central air conditioner?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Sometimes. A 3-ton condenser usually runs on 3,000 to 4,000 watts but can need several times that to start. A large portable in the 9,000-plus running watt class can often manage it, especially with a soft-start kit fitted by an HVAC technician, though little may be left for anything else. Many households cool one room with a window unit instead.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long will a generator run on a tank of gas?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Load decides it. A small inverter can run eight hours or more at light load on about a gallon, while a large open-frame portable might manage eight to twelve hours at half load on a big tank and much less near full load. Look up the manufacturer's run-time figures at 25 and 50 percent load and plan your fuel around realistic use.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a battery power station a real replacement for a generator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For short outages, apartments and medical devices, yes, and it is the only backup you can run indoors. For multi-day outages it supplements a generator rather than replacing one, unless solar panels or a generator recharge it. A typical refrigerator uses 1 to 2 kWh a day, so divide the station's capacity by your daily loads to estimate how long it lasts.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

